# trigger-autofire -- flips trigger-test into STARTED at boot.
# The demo used to leave trigger-test hanging in STARTING so operators
# could see the triggered-service state; that turned into "waste of
# time" once nobody wanted to `slinitctl trigger trigger-test` by hand
# each boot. This one-shot scripted service runs slinitctl to fire
# the trigger the moment slinit's control socket is up.
#
# Manual demo remains available:
#   slinitctl stop trigger-test        # back to STOPPED
#   slinitctl start trigger-test       # → STARTING again
#   slinitctl trigger trigger-test     # → STARTED
# Order matters: `slinitctl trigger` alone only flips the trigger
# flag; the service still needs a start request to enter STARTING
# and consume that flag. `start` puts it in STARTING, `trigger`
# unblocks the transition to STARTED. Doing them in one process
# with && guarantees the trigger lands only if the start request
# went through.
#
# `--no-wait` is required, not cosmetic. `slinitctl start` waits for
# the service to reach a terminal state (dinit semantics, so the exit
# code means something). A triggered service deliberately sits in
# STARTING until its trigger fires — so waiting here would deadlock:
# start waits for started, started needs the trigger, and the trigger
# is the next command in this very line.
type = scripted
depends-on: system-init
command = /bin/sh -c "slinitctl --no-wait start trigger-test && slinitctl trigger trigger-test"
stop-command = /bin/sh -c "slinitctl untrigger trigger-test; slinitctl stop trigger-test"
